# Ops rules for agents in this repo

<!-- Copy to CLAUDE.md at the repo root. AGENTS.md.example says the same thing for
     other agent tools; keep the two in step. Replace every <PLACEHOLDER>.
     The rung line below is the only line that changes when the agent moves up a
     rung (00-ladder/ladder.md). Change it in a reviewed MR, together with the
     credential, never on its own. -->

You are working with `<TEAM_NAME>`'s ops and platform stack: Zabbix, ELK, Grafana,
GitLab CI, Argo CD with Helm, and Kubernetes. Current rung: **Read**
(see `00-ladder/ladder.md`). The rung changes only when this file changes.

## Default: read-only

- Treat every system as read-only unless this file says otherwise.
- Never write to production, by any route: no kubectl, helm or argocd changes, no
  API calls that create, update or delete, no edits on a production host.
- If a tool or command can change state, do not run it. Describe it instead.

## Changes arrive only as merge requests

What you may do with a change depends on the rung above:

- **Read:** do not commit, push or open an MR. Write the change you would propose —
  the diff, the evidence, the risk and the rollback — into your note or reply,
  marked *proposed, not applied*. A human decides whether it becomes an MR.
- **Recommend** (and above): propose the change as an MR in `<IAC_REPO>` on a
  branch named `agent/<short-topic>`. Claude Code asks before every `git push` and
  `glab mr create`; wait for the human's answer.

On every rung:

- The MR description (or the proposed change in your note) says: what and why, the
  evidence (queries and their results), the risk, how to roll back, and how to
  verify after deploy.
- Never merge, approve or deploy. A human reviews, merges and promotes.
- Never push to `<DEFAULT_BRANCH>` or any protected branch.

## Stop and ask

Stop and ask a human before anything destructive or hard to undo: deleting,
draining, cordoning, scaling to zero, restarting, rolling back, syncing, silencing
or acknowledging an alarm, rotating a credential, or touching data. Also stop if
you are unsure which environment you are in — confirm the cluster context and
Zabbix or Kibana instance before you query.

## How to query

- Aggregate first: counts, rates and top-N before any raw rows or log lines.
- Time-box every query to the incident window plus a margin; never "all time".
- Never dump raw logs, full dashboards or whole manifests into the conversation.
- Never print secrets, tokens, kubeconfigs or Secret objects.

## Environments

- Production: `<PROD_CONTEXTS>` — read only, always.
- Non-production: `<NONPROD_CONTEXTS>` — still read only at this rung.
- Escalation: `<ON_CALL_CHANNEL>`, owner `<AGENT_OWNER>`.

If a hook or permission rule blocks you, do not look for another way round it.
Say what you wanted to do and why, and propose it as an MR (or, at Read, as a
proposed change in your note) or ask a human.
